WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.290
A student left this note on

2
00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:03.270
my desk this morning.

3
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:05.650
It looks like nonsense.

4
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:10.420
But it’s a secret message, and by the end

5
00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.120
of this video, you’ll be able to read it.

6
00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:16.530
Today: how encryption keeps

7
00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.990
secrets, from an ancient cipher

8
00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:20.470
to the apps on your phone.

9
00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:24.130
Encryption scrambles a message so that

10
00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:26.430
only someone with the key can read it.

11
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.560
The readable message is called plaintext,

12
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.160
and the scrambled one is ciphertext.

13
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.300
Turning it back is called decryption.

14
00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:39.090
One of the oldest is Caesar’s cipher,

15
00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:40.850
named after the Roman general.

16
00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:43.350
You shift every letter along the

17
00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.890
alphabet by the same number.

18
00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:48.040
With a shift of three, A becomes

19
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.080
D, B becomes E, and so on.

20
00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.090
The shift is the key.

21
00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:56.450
But Caesar’s cipher has a big

22
00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:59.170
weakness: there are very few keys.

23
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.520
In English there are only twenty-five

24
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.800
useful shifts, and in Arabic,

25
00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:05.820
twenty-seven.

26
00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.380
Anyone can simply try them all,

27
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.820
in a few minutes by hand, or

28
00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:12.180
instantly with a computer.

29
00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.900
There’s an even smarter way, first

30
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.100
described more than a thousand years ago

31
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.480
by Al-Kindi, a scholar in Baghdad.

32
00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:22.910
Some letters are far more common

33
00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:25.370
than others: in English it’s E,

34
00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:27.710
and in Arabic, alif.

35
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.700
Count the letters in a secret

36
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.900
message, and the most common one

37
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.880
probably stands for E, or alif.

38
00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:36.600
Quick check!

39
00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.360
Using Caesar’s cipher with a shift of one,

40
00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.840
how would you write the word CAT?

41
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.360
D, B, U.

42
00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:50.380
Each letter moves one step along: C

43
00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:55.300
becomes D, A becomes B, and T becomes U.

44
00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.810
To decrypt, you shift

45
00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.050
back by the same key.

46
00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.000
Modern encryption still uses keys, but

47
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.440
there are unimaginably many of them.

48
00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:08.310
A common key size gives a number of

49
00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.750
possible keys with thirty-nine digits.

50
00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:14.000
Even the fastest supercomputers would

51
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.180
need far longer than the age of the

52
00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:17.660
universe to try them all.

53
00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:21.000
You use encryption every day.

54
00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:24.550
Chat apps like WhatsApp use end-to-end

55
00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:27.010
encryption, so only the people in the

56
00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:28.370
chat can read the messages.

57
00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:32.730
A web address that starts with https means

58
00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.710
your connection to the site is encrypted.

59
00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:37.690
And most modern phones encrypt

60
00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:39.670
their storage, with your screen

61
00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:41.070
lock protecting the key.

62
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.960
Encryption is strong, but it can’t

63
00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:46.480
protect a key you give away.

64
00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.240
That’s why attackers trick people

65
00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:51.760
for codes and passwords instead

66
00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:52.780
of breaking the maths.

67
00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:54.710
Keep your screen lock,

68
00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:56.910
passwords and codes to yourself.

69
00:02:58.290 --> 00:02:59.030
Your turn.

70
00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:03.390
Why is Caesar’s cipher so easy to crack?

71
00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:07.290
Is it because it’s old, or because there

72
00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:10.110
are so few keys that you can try them all?

73
00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.300
So few keys that you can try

74
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.200
them all, and counting letters

75
00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:16.980
gives it away even faster.

76
00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.740
Remember: encryption needs a key, modern

77
00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:23.960
keys are too many to try, and a key you

78
00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:25.680
share isn’t secret any more.

79
00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:29.220
Try it tomorrow: write a short message

80
00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.660
to a friend in Caesar’s cipher, and

81
00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:32.960
tell them the key in person.

82
00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:34.570
And the student’s note?

83
00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:37.220
Shift each letter back by three.

84
00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.340
It says: Hello from Amira.
